1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,138
There's a storm on Jupiter so big that Earth would fit inside it.

2
00:00:05,138 --> 00:00:08,276
It has been raging since at least the 1800s.

3
00:00:08,276 --> 00:00:11,286
And it's only number five on this list.

4
00:00:11,286 --> 00:00:14,006
Because out there are objects so huge,

5
00:00:14,006 --> 00:00:18,475
so dense and so violent that they barely seem possible.

6
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:21,786
From a mountain three times taller than Everest,

7
00:00:21,786 --> 00:00:25,565
to light that has been travelling for thirteen and a half billion years.

8
00:00:25,565 --> 00:00:29,210
These are seven of the most extreme things in the universe.

9
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,438
Number seven.

10
00:00:36,438 --> 00:00:37,400
Olympus Mons.

11
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:42,094
On Mars, there's a volcano that makes Everest look tiny.

12
00:00:42,094 --> 00:00:45,986
It rises about twenty-five kilometres above the plains around it.

13
00:00:45,986 --> 00:00:49,795
That's nearly three Mount Everests stacked on top of each other.

14
00:00:49,795 --> 00:00:51,370
And it's not just tall.

15
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:56,136
Its base is about six hundred kilometres wide, roughly the size of Arizona,

16
00:00:56,136 --> 00:01:00,070
and its edges drop away in cliffs up to six kilometres high.

17
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:01,659
How did it get so big?

18
00:01:01,659 --> 00:01:04,200
Mars has no moving tectonic plates.

19
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,310
So the volcano sat over the same hot spot,

20
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:10,492
piling up lava again and again, for an incredibly long time.

21
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:14,980
Number six.

22
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:17,707
A storm with straight edges.

23
00:01:17,707 --> 00:01:20,769
Nature almost never makes perfect shapes this big.

24
00:01:20,769 --> 00:01:21,939
But at Saturn's north pole,

25
00:01:21,939 --> 00:01:26,569
there's a six-sided jet stream about thirty thousand kilometres across.

26
00:01:26,569 --> 00:01:29,462
You could line up more than two Earths inside it.

27
00:01:29,462 --> 00:01:32,849
Winds race around it at over three hundred kilometres an hour,

28
00:01:32,849 --> 00:01:36,167
with a giant hurricane spinning at the very centre.

29
00:01:36,167 --> 00:01:39,469
The Voyager probes first spotted it in the early 1980s.

30
00:01:39,469 --> 00:01:43,286
Decades later, when Cassini arrived, it was still there.

31
00:01:43,286 --> 00:01:48,415
Scientists think it forms where winds of different speeds meet and bend the jet stream into waves.

32
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:52,437
Number five.

33
00:01:52,437 --> 00:01:54,227
The Great Red Spot.

34
00:01:54,227 --> 00:01:56,874
This storm on Jupiter is wider than our entire planet.

35
00:01:56,874 --> 00:01:59,128
When NASA measured it in 2017,

36
00:01:59,128 --> 00:02:01,918
it was about sixteen thousand kilometres across.

37
00:02:01,918 --> 00:02:04,007
That's one point three Earths.

38
00:02:04,007 --> 00:02:04,843
But here's the twist.

39
00:02:04,843 --> 00:02:07,318
It's shrinking.

40
00:02:07,318 --> 00:02:10,753
In the nineteenth century, it was well over two Earths wide.

41
00:02:10,753 --> 00:02:16,216
NASA's Juno spacecraft also found that its roots plunge more than three hundred kilometres down.

42
00:02:16,216 --> 00:02:19,044
That's fifty to a hundred times deeper than Earth's oceans.

43
00:02:22,033 --> 00:02:23,364
Number four.

44
00:02:23,364 --> 00:02:26,159
A sugar cube you could never lift.

45
00:02:26,159 --> 00:02:27,307
When a giant star dies,

46
00:02:27,307 --> 00:02:31,219
its core can collapse into a neutron star.

47
00:02:31,219 --> 00:02:34,282
Around one and a half times the mass of our Sun,

48
00:02:34,282 --> 00:02:37,759
crushed into a ball about the size of a city.

49
00:02:37,759 --> 00:02:41,671
This is the densest material we can directly observe.

50
00:02:41,671 --> 00:02:46,469
A piece the size of a sugar cube would weigh about a billion tonnes on Earth.

51
00:02:46,469 --> 00:02:48,746
That's as much as a mountain.

52
00:02:48,746 --> 00:02:52,327
And some neutron stars spin hundreds of times every second,

53
00:02:52,327 --> 00:02:56,193
sweeping beams of radiation across space like a cosmic lighthouse.

54
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:01,939
Number three takes that and turns it up.

55
00:03:01,939 --> 00:03:03,113
A magnetar.

56
00:03:03,113 --> 00:03:08,401
This is a neutron star with the strongest magnetic field known in the universe.

57
00:03:08,401 --> 00:03:10,071
Put one a sixth of the way to the Moon,

58
00:03:10,071 --> 00:03:13,410
and it would wipe every credit card on Earth.

59
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:15,511
On December twenty-seventh, 2004,

60
00:03:15,511 --> 00:03:20,265
a magnetar called SGR 1806-20 erupted.

61
00:03:20,265 --> 00:03:23,781
It was about fifty thousand light-years away, across the galaxy.

62
00:03:23,781 --> 00:03:29,044
And the flash still reached Earth strongly enough to light up our upper atmosphere.

63
00:03:29,044 --> 00:03:30,451
In just a tenth of a second,

64
00:03:30,451 --> 00:03:35,589
it released more energy than our Sun gives off in one hundred and fifty thousand years.

65
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:39,655
Number two.

66
00:03:39,655 --> 00:03:43,193
The first black hole humanity ever photographed.

67
00:03:43,193 --> 00:03:47,514
In 2019, a team linking radio telescopes across the planet,

68
00:03:47,514 --> 00:03:50,074
the Event Horizon Telescope, revealed this.

69
00:03:50,074 --> 00:03:54,373
The shadow of the black hole at the heart of the galaxy M87.

70
00:03:54,373 --> 00:03:56,414
It's fifty-five million light-years away,

71
00:03:56,414 --> 00:03:59,744
and it holds the mass of six and a half billion Suns.

72
00:03:59,744 --> 00:04:02,859
Its event horizon, the point of no return,

73
00:04:02,859 --> 00:04:05,925
is just under forty billion kilometres across.

74
00:04:05,925 --> 00:04:07,124
Drop it into our solar system,

75
00:04:07,124 --> 00:04:11,358
and it would swallow the orbits of every single planet, with room to spare.

76
00:04:14,367 --> 00:04:15,724
And number one.

77
00:04:15,724 --> 00:04:18,865
The most distant galaxy ever confirmed.

78
00:04:18,865 --> 00:04:20,892
It's called MoM-z14,

79
00:04:20,892 --> 00:04:25,695
and the James Webb Space Telescope caught its light in 2025.

80
00:04:25,695 --> 00:04:30,161
That light has been travelling for about thirteen and a half billion years.

81
00:04:30,161 --> 00:04:35,262
It left this galaxy when the universe was only around two hundred and eighty million years old.

82
00:04:35,262 --> 00:04:38,245
That's about two percent of its age today.

83
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:41,176
So you're not seeing this galaxy as it is now.

84
00:04:41,176 --> 00:04:44,212
You're seeing it as a baby, at the dawn of the universe,

85
00:04:44,212 --> 00:04:47,518
long before the Sun or the Earth existed.

86
00:04:47,518 --> 00:04:49,368
And it's surprisingly bright,

87
00:04:49,368 --> 00:04:52,889
brighter than many astronomers expected a galaxy that young could be.

88
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:58,688
A mountain, a storm, a hexagon, a sugar cube,

89
00:04:58,688 --> 00:05:03,188
a flash, a shadow, and a speck of ancient light.

90
00:05:03,188 --> 00:05:05,780
Every single one is real.

91
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:07,553
Which one surprised you the most?

92
00:05:07,553 --> 00:05:08,899
Tell us in the comments.

93
00:05:08,899 --> 00:05:12,290
And if you want more of the universe at its most extreme,

94
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:13,209
you know what to do.
